 think the success  of cop 26 coming out and just showing  how much more of the world is paying  attention to this is starting to come  out in these communiques from the from  the west um from all these different  groups and  you know i think we're going to keep  seeing it more and more  especially with the fact that climate  change we're seeing storms becoming more  severe becoming more frequent when we  start to look forward into the future  and as vikki was talking about some of  the gdp numbers and economic forecasts  going forward we're going to see that  start to make greater and greater  impacts across the board and around the  world  and and john on the point of whether or  not it could change policy these kind of  surveys that they influence politicians  absolutely you know i i think on the  other hand anything that happens locally  tends to influence politicians more than  anything that happens globally which is  why we do see a bit of this tragedy of  the commons with climate change as the  united states you know whether it's  going to be a congress person or the  president starts to see more of more of  this happen we're going to see more  action now we have seen in the united  states there's a there's a build back  better built that's been um in congress  for for quite a while and we're  disappointed to see that it hasn't gone  through yet but we're also enlightened  to see the fact that it's still on the  table and and we're hopeful  and vicky what about the uk because uk  um experts were much more worried about  the failure of cyber security than  climate change and what does that tell  us about the uk's approach do you think  i don't think it means that businesses  are not interested or the experts are  not really interested or worried about  the climate change issues it means that  there are some pressing  uh problems that need to be dealt with  and i think the  the worries that they're not addressed  uh certainly not by politicians or by  regulation and the reason for it i think  if you look at the list of countries  that have been most affected by cyber  attacks i mean yes we hear quite a lot  of sort of political cyber attacks that  take place in in various parts of the  world but in reality in terms of  businesses and the way in which they've  been affected it seems to be mostly in  the us and number two in the uk so again  it's something that businesses will be  facing increasingly and if you uh  perhaps focus too much on let's say just  climate change or maybe another issue  which is of course you know could be  quite important right now you could miss  the fact that being able to operate  in the world economy the way that one of  the businesses need to do also means  that this should be free from those  cyber attacks so at least they should be  able to  find somewhere protecting themselves  against and i think that's what's coming  up i think in the survey okay let's talk  about our next topic now shall we  because the environment secretary george  eustis could add ketchup sachets and  mini milk cartons to a plastics ban list  according to a story that featured in  the sun newspaper today now the list has  already seen the demise of plastic  straws and stirrers but are ketchup  sachets really the obvious contender for  a ban or is it simply a petty little  project as described by one think tank  vicky what do you think should we ban a  tomato ketchup sachets or should the  government be focusing on bigger issues  when it comes to the environment  i think  we could focus on bigger issues but you  can also do little things that might  make a difference in my view you know  going down this route isn't going to  really suddenly you know change the  climate change projections that that  we've got but of course every little bit  helps but if we do this here in the uk  or another little thing that makes  people feel happier perhaps that  something is being done at a micro level  the real question is is this a 