 matter which  products are targeted  well yes i think it needs to be seen  that it's not necessarily sort of  discriminating against particular parts  of the economy and i think this is a  question that needs to be addressed in  all sorts of areas for example you know  if i can digress a second and talk about  electric vehicles for example yes and  we're pushing those quite hard but in  reality quite a lot of people cannot  afford them they don't have the charging  points so and there is a feeling that  perhaps what we're doing is we're making  it much harder for those of the lower  end of the paid bracket and making it  easier for those higher up john a quick  word on what you'd like to see banned  yeah i think more rather than ban i  think we need more information uh  consumers tend to make really good  choices and just to go back to where i  just mentioned thinking about um if  we're getting beef we want to make sure  that if it's coming from the amazon that  we have reliable data that says that  this is not from an area that was  previously pristine rainforest it was  something that was used on marginalized  land that's already in existence and the  consumers can then make the choice and  make sure that's what's on their dinner  table or what their what they're buying  from a restaurant is actually good for  the planet okay well john verdick and  vicki price really interesting to talk  to you tonight thanks very much indeed  for your time  thank you  well tomorrow i'll be joined by xihu  honorary fellow at oxford's  environmental change institute and  richard black senior associate at the  energy and climate intelligence unit  thanks for watching i'll see you next  time  [Music]
 

